Chapter: Type Environment
  cites Foreword chapter Sort

 An immutable mapping from names to types.

Section: Environment Type
  TypeEnv = record {
   bindings : List TypeBinding,
   locals : List TypeBinding,
   chapter-renames : List RenameEntry,
   const-ranges : List ConstRange,
   local-ranges : List ConstRange
  }

  TypeBinding = record {
   name : Text,
   bound-type : CodexType
  }

 A ConstRange records the proven value range of a name — a
 literal-defined constant (const-ranges) or a let-local bound to a
 proven expression (local-ranges). The range lives BESIDE the
 registered type, never in it: refining the type itself would make
 two distinct constants disjoint under unification and break
 comparisons, arithmetic, and list literals that mix them. Only the
 narrowing lint reads these tables, on its would-warn path
 (BoundsProverReach.md).

  ConstRange = record {
   name : Text,
   lo : Integer,
   hi : Integer
  }

Section: Construction
  empty-type-env : TypeEnv = TypeEnv { bindings = [], locals = [], chapter-renames = [], const-ranges = [], local-ranges = [] }

Section: Lookup (locals first, then globals)
  env-lookup-in : List TypeBinding, Text -> CodexType
  env-lookup-in (bs) (name) =
   let len = list-length bs
   in if len == 0 then ErrorTy
   else let pos = bsearch-text-pos bs name 0 len
   in if pos >= len then ErrorTy
   else let b = list-at bs pos
   in if b.name == name then b.bound-type else ErrorTy

  env-has-in : List TypeBinding, Text -> Boolean
  env-has-in (bs) (name) =
   let len = list-length bs
   in if len == 0 then False
   else let pos = bsearch-text-pos bs name 0 len
   in if pos >= len then False
      else (list-at bs pos).name == name

  env-lookup : TypeEnv, Text -> CodexType
  env-lookup (env) (name) =
   let llen = list-length (env.locals)
   in let lpos = bsearch-text-pos (env.locals) name 0 llen
   in if lpos < llen
      then let lb = list-at (env.locals) lpos
           in if lb.name == name then lb.bound-type
              else env-lookup-in (env.bindings) name
      else env-lookup-in (env.bindings) name

  env-has : TypeEnv, Text -> Boolean
  env-has (env) (name) =
   let llen = list-length (env.locals)
   in let lpos = bsearch-text-pos (env.locals) name 0 llen
   in if lpos < llen
      then if (list-at (env.locals) lpos).name == name then True
           else env-has-in (env.bindings) name
      else env-has-in (env.bindings) name

Section: Unsorted Membership (linear scan, for use before env-finalize)
  env-has-linear-in : List TypeBinding, Text, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  env-has-linear-in (bs) (name) (i) (len) =
   if i == len then False
   else if (list-at bs i).name == name then True
        else env-has-linear-in bs name (i + 1) len

  env-has-unsorted : TypeEnv, Text -> Boolean
  env-has-unsorted (env) (name) =
   env-has-linear-in (env.bindings) name 0 (list-length (env.bindings))

Section: Bind (unsorted accumulation — call env-finalize before lookup)
  env-replace-in : List TypeBinding, Text, CodexType, Integer, Integer -> List TypeBinding
  env-replace-in (bs) (name) (ty) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then list-push bs (deck-record (TypeBinding { name = name, bound-type = ty }))
   else if (list-at bs i).name == name then list-set-at bs i (deck-record (TypeBinding { name = name, bound-type = ty }))
   else env-replace-in bs name ty (i + 1) len

  env-bind : TypeEnv, Text, CodexType -> TypeEnv
  env-bind (env) (name) (ty) =
   let bs = env.bindings
   in __record-set env "bindings" (env-replace-in bs name ty 0 (list-length bs))

 Binding a local yields a NEW environment. Both halves of that are
 load-bearing, and each one alone is not enough.

 `__record-set` is an in-place field store that hands back the SAME
 record (`emit-record-set-builtin`) — a controlled concession that holds
 only while single ownership is threaded linearly (VisionAndVirtues,
 virtue 5). A binder does not own its caller's env: `infer-let` extends
 an env for its body and returns a CheckResult carrying no env, so the
 caller goes on using the env it passed in. Setting the field through it
 therefore wrote the binding into the CALLER's environment, and the
 local stayed visible after inference popped back out — an if-arm's
 `let` escaped its arm, and a leaked local could shadow a global at a
 later read and reject a valid program (CDX2001).

 `list-insert-at` is likewise the in-place builtin: under capacity it
 mutates the list and returns the same object. So a fresh record that
 kept the caller's list would still mutate through the shared list.
 The copy carries one slot of headroom, so the insert always lands on
 the fresh list's in-place path and never reallocates.

 The sibling table `local-ranges` conses its entries, which is why its
 LIST is safe — but it reaches the caller through `__record-set` just
 the same, so it is scoped by this constructor, not by that cons.

 Cost is one 5-field record plus a copy of (locals + 1) per bind.
 `check-all-defs` resets `locals` per def, so the copy is bounded by one
 def's binders in scope, not by the unit.

  env-bind-local : TypeEnv, Text, CodexType -> TypeEnv
  env-bind-local (env) (name) (ty) =
   let len = list-length (env.locals)
   in let pos = bsearch-text-pos (env.locals) name 0 len
   in deck-record (TypeEnv {
    bindings = env.bindings,
    locals = list-insert-at (copy-list-with-headroom (env.locals) 1) pos (deck-record (TypeBinding {
     name = name,
     bound-type = ty
    })),
    chapter-renames = env.chapter-renames,
    const-ranges = env.const-ranges,
    local-ranges = env.local-ranges
   })

Section: Constant Ranges (proven values of literal-defined constants)

 The table is appended during def registration and scanned linearly,
 but only on the narrowing lint's would-warn path, so the scan cost
 is bounded by the handful of stores that fail the type-range check.

  env-add-const-range : TypeEnv, Text, Integer -> TypeEnv
  env-add-const-range (env) (name) (value) =
   __record-set env "const-ranges" (list-push (env.const-ranges) (deck-record (ConstRange {
    name = name,
    lo = value,
    hi = value
   })))

  env-const-find : List ConstRange, Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  env-const-find (cs) (name) (i) (len) =
   if i == len then 0 - 1
   else if (list-at cs i).name == name then i
   else env-const-find cs name (i + 1) len

  env-is-local : TypeEnv, Text -> Boolean
  env-is-local (env) (name) =
   let llen = list-length (env.locals)
   in let lpos = bsearch-text-pos (env.locals) name 0 llen
   in if lpos < llen then (list-at (env.locals) lpos).name == name
   else False

 A let-local's proven range is recorded when its binding value proves
 narrower than the full i64 range. New entries prepend, and lookup
 returns the FIRST match, so an inner binding that shadows an outer
 name wins. The table is scoped by env threading: it exists only
 within the let body where the local is bound, and is discarded when
 inference pops back out.

 Consing the entry is what makes an inner binding shadow an outer one,
 but the cons alone never made the table SCOPED: reaching the caller's
 record through `__record-set` handed the new list back to the caller
 (see `env-bind-local`), so a range recorded inside a let outlived it.
 The scoping this prose claims is the fresh record, not the cons.

  env-add-local-range : TypeEnv, Text, Integer, Integer -> TypeEnv
  env-add-local-range (env) (name) (lo) (hi) =
   deck-record (TypeEnv {
    bindings = env.bindings,
    locals = env.locals,
    chapter-renames = env.chapter-renames,
    const-ranges = env.const-ranges,
    local-ranges = deck-record (ConstRange { name = name, lo = lo, hi = hi }) :: (env.local-ranges)
   })

  env-local-find : List ConstRange, Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  env-local-find (cs) (name) (i) (len) =
   if i == len then 0 - 1
   else if (list-at cs i).name == name then i
   else env-local-find cs name (i + 1) len

Section: Finalize (sort bindings for bsearch lookup)
  compare-type-binding-names : TypeBinding, TypeBinding -> Integer
  compare-type-binding-names (a) (b) = text-compare (a.name) (b.name)

  env-finalize : TypeEnv -> TypeEnv
  env-finalize (env) =
   __record-set env "bindings" (sort-by (env.bindings) compare-type-binding-names)

Section: Builtins

 The spawn family is effect-polymorphic in its callback (ForAllEff
 over the callback's row): the closure runs in the CHILD process, so
 its effects are not the caller's. Spawning itself is Concurrent.
 The child inherits the parent's capability word, so an effect the
 parent never declared is ungranted in the child too and its syscall
 is refused — the leak is fail-closed at the kernel, not silent.

 identity-set-proc writes another process's identity label, so its
 kernel helper tests two bits: identity and capability-admin. The
 row names both effects, which is what lets the manifest grant both.

 Reading an identity is an Identity effect too. identity-whoami and
 identity-get-proc read a label — your own and another process's —
 and device-seed reads the per-device entropy the key is derived
 from. All three name Identity, so a caller must declare it and the
 manifest must grant it. The row is not decoration: device-seed's
 kernel helper already tested cap-identity while its type claimed
 purity, so a caller that did not declare Identity was denied at the
 gate and got -1 back — indistinguishable from the -1 that an
 out-of-range index returns. A gated builtin with an empty row is a
 silent denial waiting to happen; that is the reason these rows are
 written down.

  identity-admin-row : EffectRow
  identity-admin-row = EffectRow {
   labels = [
    EffectLabel { name = Name { value = "Identity" }, scope = "" },
    EffectLabel { name = Name { value = "Capability" }, scope = "" }
   ],
   tail-name = "",
   tail-id = __narrow (-1)
  }

 The environment the checker starts from. Every binding comes from the
 `builtins` table (Chapter: Builtins); nothing is written out here.

 The fold is in table order and `env-bind` REPLACES, which is exactly what
 the let-chain this replaced did -- so a name bound twice still resolves to
 the last one. `process-exit` is bound twice and that is BACKLOG 2.29; the
 behaviour is preserved deliberately, because changing it is a decision and
 not a refactor.

  builtin-type-env : TypeEnv
  builtin-type-env = builtin-env-fold builtins 0 (list-length builtins) empty-type-env

  builtin-env-fold : List BuiltinSpec, Integer, Integer, TypeEnv -> TypeEnv
  builtin-env-fold (bs) (i) (len) (env) =
   if i >= len then env
   else let b = list-at bs i
   in when b.bs-type
    is Just (ty) -> builtin-env-fold bs (i + 1) len (env-bind env (b.bs-name) ty)
    is otherwise -> builtin-env-fold bs (i + 1) len env

